## 3. Physical Limits, Architectural Constraints & Economic Counter-Theories (Future Implications)

### 3.1 Physical and Economic Constraints Governing Acceleration
Future projections must incorporate structural real-world constraints (Hennessy & Patterson, 2017; Gordon, 2016):

1. **Dennard Scaling Breakdown & Thermal Dissipation (Future Implications & Strategic Horizons Monograph (2026–2035+))**: Sub-threshold leakage current ($I_{} \propto \exp((V_{gs}-V_{th})/nV_T)$) prevents voltage scaling below $\sim 0.7$, creating Dark Silicon thermal limits (Dennard et al., 1974).
2. **Landauer's Limit**: Thermodynamic bit erasure dissipates $E_{} = k_B T \ln 2 \approx 2.87 \times 10^{-21}$ at $300$ (Landauer, 1961). Radiative dissipation caps maximum compute density per cubic meter.
3. **Nordhaus Solow Paradox & Capital Substitution**: Economists note that while capital substitution increases capital share $\alpha$ in $Y = A K^\alpha L^{1-\alpha}$, regulatory friction and physical construction latencies slow real GDP growth to $23\%$ (Nordhaus, 2007, 2021).


## 4. Strategic Recommendations & Policy Horizons (2026–2035+) (Future Implications)
To navigate the exponential transition, policy-makers and institutional leaders must adopt a three-pillar adaptation strategy: (1) Siting Gigawatt-scale compute hubs directly adjacent to dedicated nuclear/zero-carbon energy assets, (2) Establishing sovereign semiconductor supply chain redundancies for High-NA EUV lithography and HBM packaging, and (3) Structuring fiscal policies to buffer labor market transitions as capital income share expands.


